# shellcheck shell=bash
#
# shared/artefact.lib -- reading an Intent artefact's own provenance. SOURCED, never run:
# it is 0644 precisely so `is_handler` (-f AND -x) cannot mistake it for a command.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN `lib/helpers`, WHICH IS WHERE I PUT IT FIRST AND WAS
# WRONG. `lib/helpers` is DEVBIN-OWNED -- vendored from the devbin install that
# `manifest.sha256` names as its `source:`, and checksummed there. Putting an
# Intent-specific parser there made `devbin doctor` report `edited:` (driven,
# rc=1) and would have let a `devbin upgrade` overwrite the file and DELETE this
# function, breaking its call sites in `cmd/macos` with nothing saying why. The
# manifest states the rule in its own header -- "Files not listed here --
# config.yaml, cmd/, help/ -- belong to the project" -- so `cmd/` is the project
# territory and `lib/` is not.
#
# artefact_source_commit <mach-o> -- what the ARTEFACT says it was built from.
#
# THE ONE EXTRACTION SITE -- TRUE AS OF 2026-08-22, AND IT WAS FALSE ON THE DAY
# THIS LINE WAS WRITTEN. The sentence used to stand alone, and it was a claim
# nobody had measured. `grep -rn 'intent-source-commit'` over every `.sh`, `.rs`
# and `.bats` found other shell implementations of one contract: inline copies
# in `self_provenance_check.sh`, `interrupt_rig.sh` and
# `provenance_fields_check.sh` -- the last being `marker_of()`, BYTE-FOR-BYTE
# this function under a different name, written by the author of this file ON
# THE DAY BEFORE they wrote this header.
#
# **A HIGHLANDER CLAIM IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A PROMISE.** One that was false when
# written is worse than none at all, because the header is where a reader stops
# looking: the next person to change the marker's shape reads THE ONE EXTRACTION
# SITE, edits one file, and ships two parsers that disagree about one string --
# which is the exact failure the sentence was written to prevent.
#
# CONSUMERS, NAMED SO THE CLAIM IS CHECKABLE RATHER THAN ASSERTED (if you add
# one, add it here; if this list and `grep` ever disagree, `grep` is right):
#   - `bin/.devbin/cmd/macos`
#   - `bin/.devbin/cmd/local`
#   - `bin/.devbin/cmd/shared/currency.lib` (`artefact_pair_marker`), and
#     through it `cmd/cli` and the build arms that source it
#   - `intent/st/ST0056/parity/tools/self_provenance_check.sh`
#   - `intent/st/ST0056/parity/tools/interrupt_rig.sh`
#   - `intent/st/ST0056/parity/tools/provenance_fields_check.sh`
#
# Each parity tool HARD STOPS at exit 2 if it cannot read this file. There is no
# `source || define-my-own` fallback (vc's ruling, 2026-08-22): a fallback
# restores the duplicate at exactly the moment nobody is watching.
#
# Embedded by `build.rs` (AC-11.5) and read back out of the binary; `strings` and
# nothing else, so this asks the bytes rather than any record shipped beside them.
#
# The marker is SELF-DELIMITING and that is load-bearing here rather than
# cosmetic: rodata packs string literals with no separator, so an unterminated
# marker runs into whatever the linker laid down next -- measured during this
# row's canary as `intent-source-commit:<sha>unsafe`, with `unsafe` belonging to
# an unrelated literal. The `[^]]*` stops at the bracket the artefact provides.
#
# Returns 1 and prints nothing when `strings` read the file and found no marker,
# so "cannot say" is a distinct answer from any value. A binary built before the
# embed landed has no marker at all, and that is exactly the state this must not
# read as a pass.
#
# **RETURNS 2, WITH THE REFUSAL AND A REMEDY ON STDERR, WHEN `strings` COULD NOT
# READ THE FILE AT ALL** -- absent from PATH, refused by the Xcode shim, or a
# path that is not there. Until 2026-09-15 that case went to /dev/null and
# returned 1, so an unreadable binary and a binary with no marker gave the same
# answer: measured with `strings` taken off PATH, a marked release binary came
# back rc=1 with no output. Every consumer named above keeps its no-marker answer
# for rc 1 and never prints or records one for rc 2: a caller that decides stops
# non-zero with this refusal already printed, and a diagnostic says the binary was
# not read (vc's ruling, 2026-09-15).
#
# The scan goes through a file rather than a pipe so each tool's exit status is
# its own: `strings` piped into `head` can die of SIGPIPE on a marker found
# early, and `pipefail` would report that success as a failure.
artefact_source_commit() {
  local bin="$1" scan rc marker
  scan="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/artefact-strings.XXXXXX")" || {
    printf 'error: no temporary file to read %s through\n  remedy: make %s writable, or point TMPDIR at a directory that is\n' "$bin" "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}" >&2
    return 2
  }
  if strings "$bin" > "$scan" 2>&1; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # The backticks are literal: they quote a command for the reader to run.
    # shellcheck disable=SC2016
    printf 'error: strings could not read %s (rc=%s), so its source-commit marker is unknown, not absent\n  caused by: %s\n  remedy: run `strings %s` to see the refusal; `strings` ships with the Xcode command line tools (`xcode-select --install`)\n' \
      "$bin" "$rc" "$(head -3 "$scan" | tr '\n' ' ')" "$bin" >&2
    rm -f "$scan"
    return 2
  fi
  if marker="$(grep -o -m 1 '\[intent-source-commit:[^]]*\]' "$scan")"; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi
  rm -f "$scan"
  case "$rc" in
    0) ;;
    1) return 1 ;;
    *)
      printf 'error: grep could not scan the strings of %s (rc=%s)\n  remedy: re-run; a scan of a file this function just wrote has no expected failure\n' "$bin" "$rc" >&2
      return 2
      ;;
  esac
  marker="${marker%%$'\n'*}"
  marker="${marker#\[intent-source-commit:}"
  printf '%s' "${marker%\]}"
}
